    38	## Statement refuted
    39	
    40	**Refuted:** that a continuous image of a locally compact space is locally
    41	compact ([[def-locally-compact-space]]). Local compactness is not preserved by
    42	continuous maps, and it is not even preserved by quotient maps.
    43	
    44	**Witness.** Write $\iota : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ for the canonical natural
    45	([[def-canonical-natural]]) and put $N := \{\, \iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$,
    46	the set of naturals inside $\mathbb{R}$. Let
    47	
    48	$$Y \;:=\; (\mathbb{R} \setminus N) \cup \{N\}, \qquad q : \mathbb{R} \to Y, \quad q(t) := t \ (t \notin N), \quad q(t) := N \ (t \in N),$$
    49	
    50	and give $Y$ the quotient topology of $q$ ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then $q$ is
    51	a continuous surjection, $\mathbb{R}$ is locally compact, and $Y$ is **not**
    52	locally compact: the point $\ast := N$ of $Y$ has no compact neighbourhood.
    53	
    54	## Facts & Assumptions
    55	
    56	**Given:** $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, the set $N = \{\iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, the set $Y$, the surjection $q$, and the quotient topology on $Y$.
