items/def-locally-compact-space.md:35:---
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:36:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:37:## Definition
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:38:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:39:A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is **locally
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:40:compact** when
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:41:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:42:> every point of $X$ has a compact neighbourhood:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:43:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:44:that is, for every $x \in X$ there is a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:45:([[def-neighbourhood-top]]) that is a compact subset of $X$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:46:([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:47:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:48:**A neighbourhood need not be open here**, and that is what makes the condition
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:49:the weak one it is meant to be: $N$ is required only to contain some open set
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:50:containing $x$. Writing "compact **open** neighbourhood" instead would define a
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:51:strictly stronger property, satisfied by no space in which a point has no compact
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:52:open neighbourhood, $\mathbb{R}$ among them; and requiring the compact set merely
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:53:to *contain* $x$ would define a property so weak that every space with a
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:54:singleton has it, singletons being compact.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:55:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:56:**Every compact space is locally compact**, since $X$ itself is a neighbourhood
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:57:of each of its points and is a compact subset of itself. The converse fails, and
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:58:$\mathbb{R}^n$ is the standard witness.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:59:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:60:**What the condition says in a metric space.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:61:carrying its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]),
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:62:with balls as in [[def-metric-ball]], and let $x \in X$. Then
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:63:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:64:> $x$ has a compact neighbourhood **if and only if** there are a real $r > 0$ and
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:65:> a compact $K \subseteq X$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq K$.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:66:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:67:*Both directions are immediate and are discharged here.* If $N$ is a compact
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:68:neighbourhood of $x$, fix an open $U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$; by
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:69:[[def-metric-topology]] there is $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U \subseteq N$, so
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:70:$K := N$ serves. Conversely, if $B(x,r) \subseteq K$ with $K$ compact, then $K$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:71:contains the open set $B(x,r)$, which contains $x$, so $K$ is a neighbourhood of
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:72:$x$ and is compact. Compactness of a subset of $(X,d)$ means the same thing read
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:73:metrically and read topologically
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:74:([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]), so the criterion may be
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:75:applied with either development's theorems.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:76:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:77:**$\mathbb{R}^n$ is locally compact for every $n \ge 1$.** Give $\mathbb{R}^n$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:78:the product topology, which is the metric topology of the Euclidean metric $d_2$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:79:([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]). For
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:80:$p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the set
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:81:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:82:$$Q_p \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : d_2(x,p) \le 1 \,\}$$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:83:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:84:is closed, being the complement of the union of the open balls $B(y, d_2(y,p) - 1)$
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:85:over the points $y$ with $d_2(y,p) > 1$, and it is bounded
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:86:([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]), lying inside $B(p, 2)$; so $Q_p$ is compact by
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:87:[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]. It contains the open ball $B(p,1)$,
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:88:which contains $p$, so it is a compact neighbourhood of $p$. The space
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:89:$\mathbb{R}^n$ is not compact, so local compactness is strictly weaker than
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:90:compactness.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:91:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:92:## Remarks
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:93:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:94:**Local compactness is a local condition and compactness is not.** The definition
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:95:quantifies over points and asks for something in a neighbourhood of each; nothing
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:96:is asserted about covers of the whole space. That is why a locally compact space
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:97:may be as large as one likes, and why the two properties separate.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:98:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:99:**Where the extra strength is needed.** For an arbitrary space, "every point has
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:100:a compact neighbourhood" does not by itself give a *base* of compact
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:101:neighbourhoods at each point, nor an open set with compact closure around each
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:102:compact set. Both of those do follow once the space is also Hausdorff, and that
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:103:is [[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]; several authors build the stronger
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:104:condition into the definition and then note the agreement in the Hausdorff case.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:105:This library takes the weak definition and proves the strengthening under the
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:106:hypothesis that licenses it.
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:107:
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:108:**Local compactness is not hereditary**, unlike metrizability. A subspace of a
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:109:locally compact space need not be locally compact, and
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:110:[[fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary]] records the failure with a witness; what
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:111:does survive is heredity along open and along closed subspaces of a locally
items/def-locally-compact-space.md:112:compact **Hausdorff** space ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]).
