items/def-quotient-topology.md:41:---
items/def-quotient-topology.md:42:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:43:## Definition
items/def-quotient-topology.md:44:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:45:**The quotient topology.** Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space
items/def-quotient-topology.md:46:([[def-topological-space]]), let $Y$ be a set and let $q : X \to Y$ be a
items/def-quotient-topology.md:47:surjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). The **quotient topology** on
items/def-quotient-topology.md:48:$Y$ induced by $q$ is the final topology of the one-element family $(q)$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:49:([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]):
items/def-quotient-topology.md:50:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:51:$$\mathcal{T}_q \;:=\; \{\, V \subseteq Y : q^{-1}[V] \in \mathcal{T} \,\} .$$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:52:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:53:That this is a topology is discharged in [[def-initial-and-final-topology]],
items/def-quotient-topology.md:54:where every final topology is verified to satisfy (T1), (T2) and (T3). Dually,
items/def-quotient-topology.md:55:$C \subseteq Y$ is closed in $\mathcal{T}_q$ exactly when $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed
items/def-quotient-topology.md:56:in $X$, because $q^{-1}[Y \setminus V] = X \setminus q^{-1}[V]$.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:57:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:58:**Quotient map.** A surjection $q : X \to Y$ between topological *spaces* is a
items/def-quotient-topology.md:59:**quotient map** (also *identification map*) when the topology of $Y$ is the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:60:quotient topology of $q$, that is when
items/def-quotient-topology.md:61:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:62:$$V \text{ is open in } Y \iff q^{-1}[V] \text{ is open in } X \qquad (V \subseteq Y).$$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:63:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:64:The implication from left to right is exactly continuity of $q$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:65:([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]] clause
items/def-quotient-topology.md:66:(b)), so a quotient map is a continuous surjection with the extra property that
items/def-quotient-topology.md:67:its topology is as *fine* as continuity permits. Equivalently, and this form is
items/def-quotient-topology.md:68:used as often: $C$ is closed in $Y$ if and only if $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed in $X$.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:69:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:70:**Saturated sets.** Let $q : X \to Y$ be a surjection. A subset $A \subseteq X$ is
items/def-quotient-topology.md:71:**saturated** with respect to $q$ when
items/def-quotient-topology.md:72:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:73:$$A \;=\; q^{-1}\big[\,q[A]\,\big] ,$$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:74:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:75:equivalently when $A$ is a union of fibres $q^{-1}[\{y\}]$, equivalently when
items/def-quotient-topology.md:76:$x \in A$ and $q(x') = q(x)$ imply $x' \in A$. The set $q^{-1}[q[A]]$ is the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:77:**saturation** of $A$, and it is the smallest saturated set containing $A$. The
items/def-quotient-topology.md:78:map $V \mapsto q^{-1}[V]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{P}(Y)$ onto the saturated
items/def-quotient-topology.md:79:subsets of $X$, with inverse $A \mapsto q[A]$, since $q$ is surjective; under it
items/def-quotient-topology.md:80:the open sets of the quotient topology correspond exactly to the **saturated open
items/def-quotient-topology.md:81:subsets of $X$**. That correspondence is the working description of the quotient
items/def-quotient-topology.md:82:topology: to know the open sets of $Y$ is to know which open subsets of $X$ are
items/def-quotient-topology.md:83:saturated.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:84:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:85:**Quotient by an equivalence relation.** Let $\sim$ be an **equivalence relation**
items/def-quotient-topology.md:86:on $X$, that is a relation that is reflexive ($x \sim x$), symmetric
items/def-quotient-topology.md:87:($x \sim x'$ implies $x' \sim x$) and transitive ($x \sim x'$ and $x' \sim x''$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:88:imply $x \sim x''$). Write $[x] := \{\, x' \in X : x' \sim x \,\}$ for the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:89:**equivalence class** of $x$; distinct classes are disjoint and their union is
items/def-quotient-topology.md:90:$X$. The **quotient set** is $X/\!\sim\ := \{\, [x] : x \in X \,\}$ and the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:91:**canonical projection** is
items/def-quotient-topology.md:92:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:93:$$\pi : X \to X/\!\sim, \qquad \pi(x) := [x] ,$$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:94:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:95:which is a surjection by construction. The set $X/\!\sim$ always carries the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:96:quotient topology of $\pi$, and $(X/\!\sim,\ \mathcal{T}_\pi)$ is called an
items/def-quotient-topology.md:97:**identification space**. A subset of $X$ is saturated for $\pi$ exactly when it
items/def-quotient-topology.md:98:is a union of equivalence classes.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:99:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:100:**A convenient special case: collapsing a subset.** For $\varnothing \ne B \subseteq X$
items/def-quotient-topology.md:101:let $\sim_B$ be the relation whose classes are $B$ itself and the singletons
items/def-quotient-topology.md:102:$\{x\}$ for $x \notin B$; this is an equivalence relation, its classes being a
items/def-quotient-topology.md:103:partition of $X$. The resulting quotient is written $X/B$, and its canonical
items/def-quotient-topology.md:104:projection is a surjection identifying all of $B$ to a single point and doing
items/def-quotient-topology.md:105:nothing else. Saturated sets for $\sim_B$ are the sets $A$ with $A \cap B \in
items/def-quotient-topology.md:106:\{\varnothing, B\}$.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:107:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:108:**Two conventions used throughout.** First, "quotient map" is a property of a map
items/def-quotient-topology.md:109:*together with the two topologies*, never of the map alone. Second, a quotient
items/def-quotient-topology.md:110:topology is determined by $q$ together with the topology on its domain, and not by
items/def-quotient-topology.md:111:the underlying pair of sets $(X,Y)$: two different surjections onto the same set
items/def-quotient-topology.md:112:can give different topologies, the same surjection gives different topologies when
items/def-quotient-topology.md:113:its domain is retopologised, and where more than one is in play the map is named.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:114:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:115:## Remarks
items/def-quotient-topology.md:116:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:117:- **The quotient construction on sets is the one this library has already used.**
items/def-quotient-topology.md:118:  The integers ([[def-integers]]) and the rationals ([[def-rationals]]) are
items/def-quotient-topology.md:119:  quotient *sets* of exactly the shape above, and the only new content here is the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:120:  topology carried along by $\pi$.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:121:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:122:- **Why saturation is the right notion.** An open $U \subseteq X$ that is not
items/def-quotient-topology.md:123:  saturated has an image $q[U]$ whose preimage is strictly larger than $U$, and
items/def-quotient-topology.md:124:  nothing forces that preimage to be open; so $q[U]$ need not be open, and indeed
items/def-quotient-topology.md:125:  a quotient map need not be an open map. That failure is recorded on this page as
items/def-quotient-topology.md:126:  a false statement and is not a defect of the construction: the quotient topology
items/def-quotient-topology.md:127:  is defined by preimages precisely because images behave badly.
items/def-quotient-topology.md:128:
items/def-quotient-topology.md:129:- **The quotient topology is the finest making $q$ continuous.** That is the
items/def-quotient-topology.md:130:  general fact about final topologies from
items/def-quotient-topology.md:131:  [[thm-initial-and-final-characteristic-properties]], read for a one-element
items/def-quotient-topology.md:132:  family, and it is what makes "as many open sets as continuity permits" precise.
items/thm-quotient-universal-property.md:1:---
items/thm-quotient-universal-property.md:2:id: thm-quotient-universal-property
items/thm-quotient-universal-property.md:3:kind: theorem
items/thm-quotient-universal-property.md:4:title: "For a quotient map $q : X \\to Y$, a map out of $Y$ is continuous iff its composite with $q$ is; a continuous map on $X$ constant on the fibres of $q$ factors uniquely through $q$; and a composite of quotient maps is a quotient map"
