items/def-compact-space.md:35:---
items/def-compact-space.md:36:## Definition
items/def-compact-space.md:37:
items/def-compact-space.md:38:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
items/def-compact-space.md:39:
items/def-compact-space.md:40:- An **open cover** of $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is a family
items/def-compact-space.md:41:  $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ of open sets with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$,
items/def-compact-space.md:42:  where $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
items/def-compact-space.md:43:- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$
items/def-compact-space.md:44:  that is itself an open cover.
items/def-compact-space.md:45:- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or
items/def-compact-space.md:46:  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
items/def-compact-space.md:47:  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
items/def-compact-space.md:48:  harmless.
items/def-compact-space.md:49:- $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite
items/def-compact-space.md:50:  subcover: for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \varnothing$ and the
items/def-compact-space.md:51:  empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
items/def-compact-space.md:52:  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
items/def-compact-space.md:53:  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
items/def-compact-space.md:54:- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the subspace
items/def-compact-space.md:55:  $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact topological space, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the
items/def-compact-space.md:56:  subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/def-compact-space.md:57:
items/def-compact-space.md:58:**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
items/def-compact-space.md:59:The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and its own open
items/def-compact-space.md:60:sets, not about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do
items/def-compact-space.md:61:agree, but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
items/def-compact-space.md:62:[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], and no item of this library may use
items/def-compact-space.md:63:the ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading makes
items/def-compact-space.md:64:compactness a property of the topological space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ alone.
items/def-compact-space.md:65:Hence it is preserved when $A$ is embedded homeomorphically as a subspace, or
items/def-compact-space.md:66:when another ambient space induces the same topology on $A$; it need not be
items/def-compact-space.md:67:preserved if the induced topology changes. This is exactly the convention already fixed for
items/def-compact-space.md:68:metric spaces by [[def-metric-compactness]], and the agreement of that
items/def-compact-space.md:69:definition with this one is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
items/def-compact-space.md:70:
items/def-compact-space.md:71:**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
items/def-compact-space.md:72:it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. Every
items/def-compact-space.md:73:space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact too: given a cover, each $x_i$
items/def-compact-space.md:74:lies in some member, and finitely many members named in this way already cover.
items/def-compact-space.md:75:So every finite space is compact, whatever its topology, and in particular the
items/def-compact-space.md:76:discrete topology on a finite set is compact while the discrete topology on an
items/def-compact-space.md:77:infinite set is not ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
items/def-compact-space.md:78:
items/def-compact-space.md:79:**The finiteness convention.** "Finite" above is the listing form. It agrees with
items/def-compact-space.md:80:the definition of finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number
items/def-compact-space.md:81:([[def-countable]]), in both directions, and the agreement is the one already
items/def-compact-space.md:82:discharged in [[def-metric-compactness]]: a nonempty set equinumerous with
items/def-compact-space.md:83:$m \ge 1$ is listable, and a set listed as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ injects into
items/def-compact-space.md:84:$\sigma(n)$ by sending $x$ to the least $i \le n$ with $a_i = x$. Neither
items/def-compact-space.md:85:direction uses a choice principle; the second selects nothing, taking a least
items/def-compact-space.md:86:index instead.
items/def-compact-space.md:87:
items/def-compact-space.md:88:**Quasicompact is not used here.** Some authors, following Bourbaki, reserve
items/def-compact-space.md:89:*compact* for a space that is both compact in the above sense and Hausdorff, and
items/def-compact-space.md:90:call the open-cover condition alone *quasicompact*. This library follows the more
items/def-compact-space.md:91:widely adopted convention: **compact** means the open-cover condition and nothing
items/def-compact-space.md:92:more, and a Hausdorff hypothesis is always written out. The fork is recorded in
items/def-compact-space.md:93:[[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]].
items/def-compact-space.md:94:
items/def-compact-space.md:95:## Remarks
items/def-compact-space.md:96:
items/def-compact-space.md:97:**Why open covers rather than covers by arbitrary sets.** Nothing in the
items/def-compact-space.md:98:definition would break if $\mathcal{U}$ were allowed to consist of arbitrary
items/def-compact-space.md:99:subsets of $X$, but the resulting notion would be uninteresting: every space is
items/def-compact-space.md:100:covered by its singletons, and only a finite space would survive. Openness of the
items/def-compact-space.md:101:members is what makes the condition a genuine restriction, and it is what
items/def-compact-space.md:102:[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]] has to keep track of when the
items/def-compact-space.md:103:ambient space changes.
items/def-compact-space.md:104:
items/def-compact-space.md:105:**A warning about the word "cover".** A family may cover $A \subseteq X$ without
items/def-compact-space.md:106:being a family of subsets of $A$: the members are open subsets of $X$ and their
items/def-compact-space.md:107:union merely contains $A$. That is the ambient reading, and it is a different
items/def-compact-space.md:108:statement from "$\mathcal{U}$ is an open cover of the space
items/def-compact-space.md:109:$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$", whose members are open subsets of $A$. Which of the two is
items/def-compact-space.md:110:meant is written out everywhere on this page.
