items/def-continuous-map-top.md:30:---
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:31:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:32:## Definition
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:33:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:34:Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:35:([[def-topological-space]]), let $f : X \to Y$ be a function and let $x \in X$.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:36:Neighbourhoods are as in [[def-neighbourhood-top]].
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:37:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:38:$f$ is **continuous at $x$** if for every neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$ in $Y$ the
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:39:preimage $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ in $X$.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:40:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:41:$f$ is **continuous** if it is continuous at every point of $X$.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:42:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:43:**The same condition with open sets only.** $f$ is continuous at $x$ if and only
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:44:if for every open $V \subseteq Y$ with $f(x) \in V$ there is an open
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:45:$U \subseteq X$ with $x \in U$ and $f[U] \subseteq V$. Indeed, if $f$ is
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:46:continuous at $x$ and $V$ is such an open set, then $V$ is a neighbourhood of
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:47:$f(x)$, so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ and contains an open $U \ni x$,
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:48:which satisfies $f[U] \subseteq V$. Conversely, given the displayed condition and
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:49:a neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$, fix open $V_0$ with $f(x) \in V_0 \subseteq V$ and
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:50:then open $U \ni x$ with $f[U] \subseteq V_0$; then $x \in U \subseteq f^{-1}[V_0] \subseteq f^{-1}[V]$,
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:51:so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. Both forms are used below and are the
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:52:same statement written twice.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:53:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:54:**Preimage, not image.** $f^{-1}[V] = \{\, x \in X : f(x) \in V \,\}$
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:55:is the preimage in the sense of [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] and is
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:56:defined for every function, invertible or not; no inverse function is being
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:57:asserted to exist. Continuity is a condition on preimages throughout, and the
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:58:corresponding conditions on images define the *open* and *closed* maps of a later
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:59:item, which are different notions.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:60:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:61:## Remarks
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:62:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:63:- **This is the metric definition when both topologies are metric topologies.**
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:64:  For metric spaces, $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ continuity at $a$
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:65:  ([[def-metric-continuity]]) says that every ball around $f(a)$ has a ball
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:66:  around $a$ mapped into it, and the balls around a point are a neighbourhood
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:67:  base there; the identification is carried out where metrizable spaces are
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:68:  defined later on this page. Nothing about a metric survives in the definition
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:69:  above: continuity is a relation between two topologies and a function, and it
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:70:  is meaningless to ask whether a function between bare sets is continuous.
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:71:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:72:- **Continuity depends on both topologies, and coarsening the target or refining
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:73:  the source only helps.** If $f$ is continuous and $\mathcal{T}_X$ is replaced
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:74:  by a finer topology, or $\mathcal{T}_Y$ by a coarser one, $f$ remains
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:75:  continuous, since each condition to be verified is weakened and each
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:76:  neighbourhood available in the source is still available. In particular every
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:77:  map out of a discrete space and every map into an indiscrete space is
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:78:  continuous ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:79:
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:80:- **Continuity at a point is strictly weaker than continuity.** A function may be
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:81:  continuous at exactly one point, and the definition above is deliberately local
items/def-continuous-map-top.md:82:  so that the sequential criteria proved later can be stated pointwise.
