items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:40:---
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:41:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:42:## Definition
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:43:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:44:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:45:$x \in X$.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:46:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:47:A set $N \subseteq X$ is a **neighbourhood of $x$** if there is an open
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:48:$U \in \mathcal{T}$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$. The family of all neighbourhoods
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:49:of $x$ is written $\mathcal{N}(x)$ and called the **neighbourhood filter** at
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:50:$x$. A neighbourhood that is itself open is an **open neighbourhood**.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:51:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:52:**Convention, and it is a live fork: in this library a neighbourhood need not be
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:53:open.** The competing convention, used by Munkres among others, defines a
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:54:neighbourhood of $x$ to be an *open* set containing $x$. Both are in current use;
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:55:this library follows the one above and writes "open neighbourhood" in full
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:56:whenever openness is wanted, so that no statement here depends on which
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:57:convention a reader brings.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:58:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:59:A family $\mathcal{B}_x \subseteq \mathcal{N}(x)$ is a **neighbourhood base at
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:60:$x$** if every neighbourhood of $x$ contains a member of $\mathcal{B}_x$: for
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:61:every $N \in \mathcal{N}(x)$ there is $B \in \mathcal{B}_x$ with $B \subseteq N$.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:62:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:63:**Four immediate consequences, established here because they are used
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:64:constantly.**
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:65:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:66:1. *$X$ is a neighbourhood of each of its points*, since $x \in X \subseteq X$
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:67:   and $X$ is open by (T1); so $\mathcal{N}(x) \ne \varnothing$ and every point
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:68:   has at least one neighbourhood base, namely $\mathcal{N}(x)$ itself.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:69:2. *A superset of a neighbourhood of $x$ is a neighbourhood of $x$*: if
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:70:   $x \in U \subseteq N \subseteq M$ with $U$ open then $x \in U \subseteq M$.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:71:3. *The intersection of two neighbourhoods of $x$ is a neighbourhood of $x$*: if
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:72:   $x \in U \subseteq N$ and $x \in V \subseteq M$ with $U, V$ open, then
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:73:   $U \cap V$ is open by (T3) and $x \in U \cap V \subseteq N \cap M$. By
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:74:   iteration the same holds for any intersection of finitely many
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:75:   neighbourhoods of $x$.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:76:4. *A set is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of each of its points.* If
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:77:   $U$ is open and $x \in U$ then $x \in U \subseteq U$. Conversely, if $N$ is a
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:78:   neighbourhood of each of its points, choose for each $x \in N$ an open $U_x$
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:79:   with $x \in U_x \subseteq N$; then $N = \bigcup_{x \in N} U_x$ is open by (T2).
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:80:   No choice principle is involved: $U_x$ may be taken to be the union of *all*
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:81:   open subsets of $N$ containing $x$, which is determined by $x$ and $N$, and is
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:82:   open by (T2).
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:83:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:84:**Basic sets give neighbourhood bases.** If $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:85:$\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]) then
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:86:$\mathcal{B}_x := \{\, B \in \mathcal{B} : x \in B \,\}$ is a neighbourhood base
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:87:at $x$ consisting of open sets. Each such $B$ is open and contains $x$, hence is
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:88:a neighbourhood of $x$; and if $N \in \mathcal{N}(x)$, fix open $U$ with
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:89:$x \in U \subseteq N$ and then $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq U$,
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:90:which gives $B \in \mathcal{B}_x$ with $B \subseteq N$. A member of
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:91:$\mathcal{B}_x$ is called a **basic neighbourhood** of $x$.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:92:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:93:## Remarks
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:94:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:95:- **The convention costs nothing and buys one thing.** Every statement of the
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:96:  form "for every neighbourhood $N$ of $x$ ... " whose predicate is preserved
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:97:  when $N$ is enlarged is equivalent to the statement with $N$ restricted to
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:98:  open neighbourhoods: every neighbourhood contains an open one, and the
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:99:  predicate then passes to the larger set. Eventual-membership and the usual
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:100:  local-existence tests have this form; an arbitrary predicate need not. What
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:101:  the wider notion buys is that $\mathcal{N}(x)$ is a filter on $X$ in the sense
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:102:  of [[def-filter]]: consequence 1 is (F1), consequence 3 is (F3), consequence 2
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:103:  is (F4), and (F2) holds because $x \in U \subseteq \varnothing$ is impossible,
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:104:  so $\varnothing$ is a neighbourhood of no point. Under the narrower convention
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:105:  the family of open sets containing $x$ fails (F4) as soon as some non-open set
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:106:  contains an open set around $x$, so the name "neighbourhood filter" would not
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:107:  be available.
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:108:
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:109:- **A neighbourhood base is not required to be closed under intersection**, and
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:110:  the bases used below usually are not; what is required is only that its members
items/def-neighbourhood-top.md:111:  be cofinal downwards among neighbourhoods.

