 succeeded in 0ms:
   115	## Remarks
   116	
   117	- **The quotient construction on sets is the one this library has already used.**
   118	  The integers ([[def-integers]]) and the rationals ([[def-rationals]]) are
   119	  quotient *sets* of exactly the shape above, and the only new content here is the
   120	  topology carried along by $\pi$.
   121	
   122	- **Why saturation is the right notion.** An open $U \subseteq X$ that is not
   123	  saturated has an image $q[U]$ whose preimage is strictly larger than $U$, and
   124	  nothing forces that preimage to be open; so $q[U]$ need not be open, and indeed
   125	  a quotient map need not be an open map. That failure is recorded on this page as
   126	  a false statement and is not a defect of the construction: the quotient topology
   127	  is defined by preimages precisely because images behave badly.
   128	
   129	- **The quotient topology is the finest making $q$ continuous.** That is the
   130	  general fact about final topologies from
   131	  [[thm-initial-and-final-characteristic-properties]], read for a one-element
   132	  family, and it is what makes "as many open sets as continuity permits" precise.
     1	---
     2	id: thm-quotient-universal-property
     3	kind: theorem
     4	title: "For a quotient map $q : X \\to Y$, a map out of $Y$ is continuous iff its composite with $q$ is; a continuous map on $X$ constant on the fibres of $q$ factors uniquely through $q$; and a composite of quotient maps is a quotient map"
     5	status: published
     6	origin: session
     7	provenance:
     8	  statement: ai-altered
