    35	---
    36	
    37	## Statement
    38	
    39	Let $q : X \to Y$ be a quotient map ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then:
    40	
    41	1. **Characteristic property.** For every space $W$ and every function
    42	   $k : Y \to W$,
    43	   $$k \text{ is continuous } \iff k \circ q \text{ is continuous} .$$
    44	2. **Factorisation.** Let $f : X \to W$ be continuous and **constant on the
    45	   fibres of $q$**, that is $q(x) = q(x')$ implies $f(x) = f(x')$. Then there is
    46	   exactly one function $\bar f : Y \to W$ with $\bar f \circ q = f$, and it is
    47	   continuous.
    48	3. **Composites.** If $q : X \to Y$ and $p : Y \to Z$ are quotient maps then
    49	   $p \circ q : X \to Z$ is a quotient map.
    50	
    51	## Facts & Assumptions
    52	
    53	**Given:** A quotient map $q : X \to Y$, a space $W$, a function $k : Y \to W$, a continuous $f : X \to W$ constant on the fibres of $q$, and a further quotient map $p : Y \to Z$.
    54	
    55	[A1] $q$ is a surjection and $V \subseteq Y$ is open exactly when $q^{-1}[V]$ is open in $X$; the topology of $Y$ is the final topology of the one-element family $(q)$ ([[def-quotient-topology]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    56	
    57	[A2] The same holds for $p : Y \to Z$ ([[def-quotient-topology]]).
    58	
    59	[L1] For a final topology of a family $(g_i)$, a map $k$ out of the space is continuous exactly when every $k \circ g_i$ is ([[thm-initial-and-final-characteristic-properties]], claim 4; [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
    60	
    61	[L2] A map of spaces is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clause (b)).
    62	
    63	[L3] Preimages compose: $(u \circ v)^{-1}[T] = v^{-1}[u^{-1}[T]]$; a composite of surjections is a surjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    64	
    65	[L4] A topology on a set is a family of subsets of it ([[def-topological-space]]).
    66	
    67	## Proof
    68	
    69	**Proof technique:** direct.
    70	
    71	1.1 By [A1] the topology of $Y$ is a final topology of the one-element family $(q)$, so [L1] gives claim 1 at once. [A1, L1]
    72	
    73	1.2 Define $\bar f := \{\, (y,w) : \text{there is } x \in X \text{ with } q(x) = y \text{ and } f(x) = w \,\}$. It is total on $Y$, since $q$ is surjective by [A1]; and it is single valued, since $q(x) = q(x')$ implies $f(x) = f(x')$ by hypothesis. So $\bar f$ is a function $Y \to W$ with $\bar f \circ q = f$. [given, A1]
    74	
    75	1.3 Any $g : Y \to W$ with $g \circ q = f$ equals $\bar f$: for $y \in Y$ pick $x$ with $q(x) = y$, available by surjectivity, and then $g(y) = f(x) = \bar f(y)$. [given, A1]
    76	
    77	1.4 $p \circ q$ is a surjection, being a composite of surjections. [A1, A2, L3]
    78	
    79	1.5 For $V \subseteq Z$: $(p \circ q)^{-1}[V] = q^{-1}[p^{-1}[V]]$ by [L3]. [L3]
    80	
    81	2.1 By step 1.2 the map $\bar f$ exists with $\bar f \circ q = f$ continuous, so $\bar f$ is continuous by step 1.1; with step 1.3 this is claim 2. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3]
    82	
    83	2.2 Let $V \subseteq Z$. If $V$ is open in $Z$ then $p^{-1}[V]$ is open in $Y$ by [L2] and [A2], hence $q^{-1}[p^{-1}[V]]$ is open in $X$ by [A1]; by step 1.5 that set is $(p \circ q)^{-1}[V]$. [step 1.5, A1, A2, L2]
    84	
    85	2.3 Conversely, if $(p \circ q)^{-1}[V]$ is open in $X$, then $q^{-1}[p^{-1}[V]]$ is open in $X$ by step 1.5, so $p^{-1}[V]$ is open in $Y$ by [A1], so $V$ is open in $Z$ by [A2]. [step 1.5, A1, A2]
    86	
    87	3.1 By steps 1.4, 2.2 and 2.3 the map $p \circ q$ is a surjection for which $V$ is open in $Z$ exactly when $(p \circ q)^{-1}[V]$ is open in $X$; that is claim 3. With steps 1.1 and 2.1 all three claims are proved. [step 1.1, step 1.4, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, A1, L4] ∎
    88	
    89	## Remarks
    90	
    91	- **Claim 2 is how every quotient space in this library is identified.** To
    92	  produce a continuous map out of an identification space one never works with
    93	  equivalence classes directly: one writes a continuous map on the original
    94	  space, checks that it does not distinguish identified points, and quotes claim
    95	  2. Both examples of gluing on the companion page are exactly this move.
    96	
    97	- **Uniqueness in claim 2 uses only surjectivity, and continuity of $\bar f$ uses
    98	  only claim 1.** Neither uses a choice principle: step 1.3 picks a preimage for
    99	  a *single* $y$ inside a proof of an equation, which is an instance of
   100	  existential instantiation and not a selection over an index set.
   101	
   102	- **Claim 3 has no analogue for open maps or for closed maps in the direction one
   103	  wants here.** A composite of quotient maps is a quotient map, and that is what
   104	  makes iterated identifications well behaved; whether a *product* of quotient
   105	  maps is a quotient map is a different question, and it is not settled at this
   106	  point in the reading order (see [[rem-constructions-this-page-stops-short-of]]).
