    32	---
    33	
    34	## Statement
    35	
    36	Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:
    37	for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$,
    38	where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$
    39	([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.
    40	
    41	## Facts & Assumptions
    42	
    43	**Given:** A complete ordered field $F$; write $A = \{\, n \cdot 1_F : n \ge 1 \,\}$ for the set of its canonical naturals.
    44	
    45	[L1] Least-upper-bound property: every nonempty $S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound $\sup S \in F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
    46	
    47	[L2] Each canonical natural satisfies $n \cdot 1_F > 0$, one has $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$, and $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > n \cdot 1_F$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).
    48	
    49	[L3] $0 < 1_F$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]).
    50	
    51	## Proof
    52	
    53	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    54	
    55	1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $F$ is not Archimedean: there is some $x \in F$ with $n \cdot 1_F \le x$ for all $n \ge 1$, that is, $x$ is an upper bound of $A$. [assume-contra]
    56	
    57	2.1 The set $A$ is nonempty, since $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F \in A$, and it is bounded above by $x$. [step 1.1, L2]
    58	
    59	3.1 By the least-upper-bound property, $A$ has a least upper bound $s = \sup A \in F$. [step 2.1, L1]
    60	
    61	4.1 Since $1_F > 0$, we have $s - 1_F < s$; as $s$ is the least upper bound, $s - 1_F$ is not an upper bound of $A$. [step 3.1, L3]
    62	
    63	5.1 Hence there is some $m \ge 1$ with $m \cdot 1_F > s - 1_F$. [step 4.1]
    64	
    65	6.1 Adding $1_F$ to both sides, $(m+1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + 1_F > s$. [step 5.1, L2]
    66	
    67	7.1 But $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \in A$, so $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \le s$ because $s$ is an upper bound of $A$, contradicting 6.1. [step 6.1, step 3.1, L2]
    68	
    69	8.1 The assumption is therefore untenable, so $F$ is Archimedean. [step 7.1, discharge-contradiction] ∎
