research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7958-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:113:## Remarks
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7959-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:114:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7960-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:115:**Why claim 1 is a transfinite induction and not an ordinary one.** The statement being proved at $\eta$ uses the statement at $\alpha$ for a single $\alpha < \eta$ produced by the cover, not at the predecessor of $\eta$, and $\eta$ may have no predecessor. What the induction of [L4] gives is exactly the right shape: the step assumes the statement below $\eta$ and proves it at $\eta$, with no separate limit clause to write.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7961-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:116:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:7962:items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:117:**$\omega_1$ separates sequential compactness from compactness.** It is sequentially compact and countably compact and not compact, so neither of those two properties implies compactness; that is the content of [[fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact]] and [[fs-countably-compact-implies-compact]], both of which take their witness from here. The reason is visible in the proof: countably many terms cannot escape from $\omega_1$, because a countable set of countable ordinals has a countable supremum, while the uncountable cover by the initial segments has no finite subfamily covering everything.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7963-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:118:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7964-items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md:119:**The hypothesis of countable choice is inherited, not added.** It enters through two cited results whose own statements carry it — [[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]] at the boundedness step, and claim 2 of [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] at the passage from sequential to countable compactness; the boundedness of an at most countable subset of $\omega_1$ is what claim 3 rests on, and everything else in the argument takes least elements.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7965-items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:34:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-7966-items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:35:## Statement
