research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9224-items/cex-limit-point-compact-without-countable-compactness.md:82:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9225-items/cex-limit-point-compact-without-countable-compactness.md:83:**What the witness does and does not separate.** It separates limit point compactness from countable compactness, and it does so for a reason that is entirely about separation of points: each point has a partner that no open set can distinguish it from, so every point of the space is a limit point of every set containing its partner. Limit point compactness is then satisfied for free.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9226-items/cex-limit-point-compact-without-countable-compactness.md:84:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9227-items/cex-limit-point-compact-without-countable-compactness.md:85:**The space is a product of two very simple spaces**, and each factor contributes one half of the behaviour: the discrete factor supplies the countable open cover with no finite subcover, and the indiscrete factor supplies the partners that make every nonempty set have a limit point.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9228:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:34:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9229:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:35:## Statement refuted
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9230:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:36:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9231:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:37:**Refuted:** that a continuous image of a locally compact space is locally
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9232:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:38:compact ([[def-locally-compact-space]]). Local compactness is not preserved by
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9233:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:39:continuous maps, and it is not even preserved by quotient maps.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9234:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:40:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9235:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:41:**Witness.** Write $\iota : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ for the canonical natural
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9236:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:42:([[def-canonical-natural]]) and put $N := \{\, \iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$,
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9237:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:43:the set of naturals inside $\mathbb{R}$. Let
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9238:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:44:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9239:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:45:$$Y \;:=\; (\mathbb{R} \setminus N) \cup \{N\}, \qquad q : \mathbb{R} \to Y, \quad q(t) := t \ (t \notin N), \quad q(t) := N \ (t \in N),$$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9240:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:46:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9241:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:47:and give $Y$ the quotient topology of $q$ ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then $q$ is
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9242:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:48:a continuous surjection, $\mathbb{R}$ is locally compact, and $Y$ is **not**
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9243:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:49:locally compact: the point $\ast := N$ of $Y$ has no compact neighbourhood.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9244:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:50:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9245:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:51:## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9246:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:52:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9247:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:53:**Given:** $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, the set $N = \{\iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, the set $Y$, the surjection $q$, and the quotient topology on $Y$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9248:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:54:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9249:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:55:[L1] A subset $V \subseteq Y$ is open exactly when $q^{-1}[V]$ is open in $\mathbb{R}$; $q$ is continuous; and a set $G \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ with $G \cap N = \varnothing$ satisfies $q^{-1}[q[G]] = G$, while a set $G \supseteq N$ satisfies $q^{-1}[q[G]] = G$ as well ([[def-quotient-topology]], [[thm-quotient-universal-property]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9250:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:56:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9251:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:57:[L2] $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open exactly when every $x \in U$ has a real $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$; $\mathbb{R}$ is metrizable and is locally compact, the set $\{t : |t - p| \le 1\}$ being a compact neighbourhood of $p$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]], [[thm-heine-borel-rn]], [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]], [[def-locally-compact-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9252:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:58:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9253:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:59:[L3] Distinct naturals have distinct canonical naturals and $\iota(n+1) = \iota(n) + 1$, so the members of $N$ are spaced at distance at least $1$; and for every real $t$ there is a natural $n$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-natural-numbers]], [[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9254:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:60:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9255:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:61:[L4] A subset $A$ is a compact subset when the subspace it carries is compact; a closed subset of a compact space is a compact subset of it; a space in which every singleton is open is discrete, and an infinite discrete space is not compact, its singletons covering it with no finite subcover ([[def-compact-space]], [[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]], [[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]], [[def-topological-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9256:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:62:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9257:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:63:[L5] $A$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ when some open set lies between them ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9258:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:64:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9259:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:65:## Counterexample
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9260:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:66:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9261:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:67:**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9262:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:68:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9263:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:69:1.1 $q$ is a continuous surjection by [L1] and $\mathbb{R}$ is locally compact by [L2], so any failure of local compactness in $Y$ refutes the claim. Suppose $K \subseteq Y$ is a compact neighbourhood of $\ast$, and fix an open $O$ of $Y$ with $\ast \in O \subseteq K$; then $G := q^{-1}[O]$ is open in $\mathbb{R}$ and contains $N$. [L1, L2, L5, construct]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9264:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:70:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9265:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:71:2.1 For $n \in \mathbb{N}$ put $\rho_n := \tfrac12 \sup \{\, r \in \mathbb{R} : 0 < r < \tfrac12 \text{ and } (\iota(n)-r, \iota(n)+r) \subseteq G \,\}$, a supremum of a nonempty set of reals bounded above by $\tfrac12$, so $0 < \rho_n < \tfrac12$ and $(\iota(n) - \rho_n, \iota(n)+\rho_n) \subseteq G$; nothing is selected, the supremum being determined by $n$ and $G$. Put $x_n := \iota(n) + \rho_n$, so that $\iota(n) < x_n < \iota(n) + \tfrac12$ and $x_n \in G$. [L2, L3, step 1.1, construct]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9266:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:72:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9267:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:73:3.1 No $x_n$ lies in $N$, since $\iota(n) < x_n < \iota(n)+1 = \iota(n+1)$ and the members of $N$ are the $\iota(m)$; and $x_n \ne x_m$ for $n \ne m$, the two lying in disjoint intervals $(\iota(n), \iota(n)+\tfrac12)$ and $(\iota(m), \iota(m)+\tfrac12)$. So $P := \{\, x_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$ is an infinite subset of $\mathbb{R} \setminus N$ and $q$ is injective on it. [L3, step 2.1]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9268:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:74:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9269:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:75:4.1 $P$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}$: a real $t$ lies in $[\iota(m), \iota(m)+1)$ for exactly one natural $m$ when $t \ge 0$ by [L3], and in $(-\infty, 0)$ otherwise; the interval $(t - \tfrac14, t + \tfrac14)$ meets at most one of the disjoint intervals $(\iota(n), \iota(n)+\tfrac12)$, hence contains at most one member of $P$, so no real is a limit point of $P$ outside $P$ and the complement of $P$ is open by [L2]. [L2, L3, step 3.1]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9270:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:76:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9271:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:77:4.2 The subspace $q[P]$ is discrete: for each $n$ the interval $I_n := (x_n - \eta_n, x_n + \eta_n)$ with $\eta_n := \tfrac12\min\{\rho_n, \tfrac12\}$ misses $N$, since $\iota(n) < x_n - \eta_n$ and $x_n + \eta_n < \iota(n)+1$, so $q^{-1}[q[I_n]] = I_n$ is open by [L1] and $q[I_n]$ is open in $Y$; and $q[I_n] \cap q[P] = \{q(x_n)\}$, because $I_n$ contains no $x_m$ with $m \ne n$. [L1, L3, step 2.1, step 3.1]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9272:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:78:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9273:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:79:5.1 $q[P]$ is closed in $Y$: its preimage is $P$ by [L1], since $P$ misses $N$, and the preimage of the complement of $q[P]$ is the complement of $P$, which is open by step 4.1, so $q[P]$ is closed by [L1]. Moreover $q[P] \subseteq q[G] = O \subseteq K$, so $q[P]$ is a closed subset of the compact $K$ and hence a compact subset of $Y$ by [L4]. [L1, L4, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.1]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9274:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:80:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9275:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:81:6.1 So $q[P]$ is an infinite discrete compact space, which [L4] forbids: its singletons form an open cover with no finite subcover. Hence $\ast$ has no compact neighbourhood in $Y$, the space $Y$ is not locally compact, and the claim that a continuous image of a locally compact space is locally compact is refuted. [L4, step 1.1, step 5.1, step 4.2] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9276:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:82:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9277:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:83:## Remarks
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9278:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:84:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9279:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:85:**Collapsing a compact set would not work, and that is the point.** If $E$ is a *compact* subset of a locally compact Hausdorff space, the quotient collapsing $E$ to a point is again locally compact at the collapsed point: claim 4 of [[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]] gives an open $V \supseteq E$ with $\overline{V}$ compact, the saturation of $V$ is $V$ itself, so its image is open and lies inside the compact image of $\overline{V}$. So a convergent sequence together with its limit is the wrong set to collapse; what is needed is an infinite **closed discrete** set, and $N$ is the simplest one.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9280:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:86:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9281:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:87:**What the failure looks like.** Every open set of $Y$ containing $\ast$ pulls back to an open set containing all of $N$, hence containing an interval around each $\iota(n)$; the points $x_n$ chosen just to the right of each $\iota(n)$ then form a closed discrete infinite set inside it, and no compact set can contain such a thing. There is no way to make the neighbourhood small, because it must be large near infinitely many separated places at once.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9282:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:88:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9283:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:89:**Compactness behaves differently.** A continuous image of a compact space is compact ([[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]], claim 1); it is only the *local* condition that fails to survive, and it fails because "locally" is a statement about each point separately and a quotient can glue infinitely many points together.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9284:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:90:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9285:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:91:**Quotient maps are the natural place to look.** A quotient map is a continuous surjection, so this also refutes the same claim for continuous surjections; and since $q$ here is a closed map with one non-singleton fibre, closedness of the map, even with a single non-singleton fibre, is not enough to restore the conclusion.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9286-items/ex-the-alexander-subbase-lemma-applied-to-a-closed-interval.md:34:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9287-items/ex-the-alexander-subbase-lemma-applied-to-a-closed-interval.md:35:## Example
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9288-items/ex-the-alexander-subbase-lemma-applied-to-a-closed-interval.md:36:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-9289-items/ex-the-alexander-subbase-lemma-applied-to-a-closed-interval.md:37:Let $L := [0,1] = \{\, t \in \mathbb{R} : 0 \le t \le 1 \,\}$ ([[def-interval]])
--
